  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 PM CDT Tue Apr 21 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nSurface high pressure centered from Ontario into the central Red\nRiver Valley will move southeastward tonight, allowing winds to\npick up as the pressure gradient increases. That gradient will\ncontinue to tighten as low pressure in the lee of the Rockies\nstarts to move into the central Dakotas by Wednesday night. An\nupper shortwave moving into the Northern Plains will push the\nsurface trough and cold front into the Red River Valley by\nThursday afternoon. Northwesterly winds with cooler air will\nmove in Friday and into the weekend. Another low pressure system\nstarts to move in from the Central Plains into the Upper\nMississippi Valley late Sunday into Monday, but a lot of\nvariation in the details.\n\n...Near critical fire weather along eastern counties this\nevening...\n\nWhile areas around Lake of the Woods are still hanging onto\nrelative humidity readings in the 20s, locations further south\nnear Bemidji, Park Rapids and Wadena have already dropped into\nthe teens. While winds are light and variable, land managers\nreport that fuels are dry enough to create near critical\nconditions based on RH alone. Rh values will begin to recover\naround sunset and should rise into the 60s and 70s percent\novernight.\n\n...Fire weather watch tomorrow...\n\nThe center of the surface high will move east and winds will\npick up tonight into tomorrow, with gusts up to 30 mph possible.\nThere will be come moisture advection, but dew points will not\nrise as fast as the temperature will. Afternoon RH values could\ndrop to 25 percent or even lower across portions of southeastern\nND and western MN. While winds are rather borderline even as\nthey increase, there is still a chance for critical fire weather\nconditions. Best probabilities of seeing both higher winds and\nlower RH values are in west central MN. Southeastern North\nDakota will see winds up to 25 mph, and probabilities of getting\ndown below 30 percent RH are around 70 percent. And there is the\npossibility of more mixing than expected. Further east, winds\nwill be lower but conditions drier and with plenty of fuels in\nthe Bemidji area from last year's wind event, put them in the\nwatch also. \n\n...Marginal risk Thursday...\n\nProbabilities from the ensemble for over 500 J/kg of surface\nbased CAPE are around 80 to 90 percent in a narrow band ahead of\nthe cold front Thursday afternoon. While chances for getting to\n1000 J/kg or above are lower, only around 40 percent, there will\nbe some very good deep layer bulk shear at 40 to 50 kts. Much\nwill depend on how showers and a few elevated storms play out\nWednesday night and Thursday morning. Still, high enough chance\nto include a mention for Marginal risk Thursday afternoon. Hail\nup to 1 inch and winds to 60 mph will be the main threat, but\nwith the boundary in the area can't completely rule out a quick\ntornado spin up.",
